re susceptible to  the serious parts of having covered and  that seems to be perhaps  the launch pad for this initiative yeah  look i i think often in history  um awful things and crises uh allow you  to sort of take stock  um and maybe refocus on some stuffs  some stuff that they always knew about  um like i say this strategy has been  going on for five years and it's taken  boris to sort of  pull it off the shelf and get it back  into working order  uh it's the beginning uh and he says  that in his  in his release but yeah i think it's  undoubtedly  covert 19 has amplified the emotions  it's amplified the science and it's  amplified the need  to make sure that our british children  um for  all the reasons are as fit as possible  uh  and ready to be as productive as  possible as you say at least a five year  long campaign  what why do you think previous  administration something in the theresa  may's in particular  uh were seen as having watered down some  of these measures  what was their thinking do you think  well i think in in hindsight i think  we started with mr cameron um i've  always been a political in how i work  and how i stand  um but i was one of the few if not the  only member of  sort of civvy life to be involved in the  creation of chapter one  um that was looking good it looked like  he had five years ahead of him  to do some good work and then of course  brexit sort of changed things  uh then it happened mrs may came in um  and didn't have the support from her  party in my opinion um  and now um we have got to crack on we  are in crises  um and um boris seems to be  i can't believe it but boris seems to be  the one that's uh  really kind of got a plan here but what  we need  is um not one thing or two things we  need to have a kind of more  environmental strategy  where there are many many things all  pointing the same direction  to make sure that our young kids um have  the best chance  and just just to give you kind of  clarity into why do we care and why is  it utterly important to everyone in this  country  uh no matter where they come from um if  you're a kid  um and you're from a poor background  you're over twice as likely to be  overweight or obese  we know that that tracks into teenage  years and early adulthood  we know that that has correlation with  low educational attainment  we know it means that on average you get  paid less you have more days absenteeism  cost the state more and end up dying  slightly younger  than your well-off counterpart so there  is a kind of uh  an inequality story here evening the  playing field and really about  making sure that we're putting as much  support where it's needed  on the point about young people and  young obese people it just gives a sense  of  emotionally how you feel when you hear  people as they have done down the years  and we've interviewed many of them on  this news channel who advanced the  argument that actually  it's about choice and it's especially  choice for people who can't afford  necessarily healthier food  i know we've been around that block so  many times but just give you a sense of  the frustration you feel uh when people  mount that particular argument  it's very easy to kind of get onto a  one-liner sort of slightly tabloid view  on a scenario  and a person uh we've got to just step  back  and really have a deep look and i think  you know what we've learned in the last  decade  um actually is that obesity is a normal  response to an abnormal environment  and that what we need to do is as a  whole community  and that's business that's the home and  the government we need to really  try to edit what we have and what we  consider normal  and that's everything from you know the  geekiness of reformulating products  and how clean our deck of ingredients  are on snacks or or dishes  um it's it's school lunches it's holiday  hunger like the  the marcus rashford story recently was a  holiday hunger  issue so what do we feed the kids that  ar